@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+# Phase 0 baseline and readiness report
+
+**Status: BLOCKED.** This is a source/build/provenance audit at `a7847eae82aaac32175c9a7d199673d5c9323bcd` on `work/instant-mod-loading`. No Windows build, loaded-DLL capture, hardware inventory, ZZMI run, fixture run, or performance measurement was performed in this environment. Source inspection is not bottleneck attribution and no acceptance gate has passed.
+
+## Build and artifact identity
+
+`StereovisionHacks.sln` is a Visual Studio 2022 solution (file metadata says VS 17.10). `DirectX11.vcxproj` targets a dynamic library, uses MSVC toolset `v143`, Windows SDK target `10.0`, and defines x64 `Zip Release`. Its output is named `d3d11.dll` under `x64\Zip Release\`. It links checked-in x64 Nektra hook and PCRE2 static libraries, Windows SDK libraries, and solution project references (`BinaryDecompiler`, `D3D_Shaders`, `DirectXTK`). The corresponding Visual Studio installation needs the C++ desktop workload, MSVC v143 x64/x86 build tools, and a Windows 10 SDK; use the actual checked-in dependency files rather than substituting library versions. This is source-derived readiness, not a successful build claim.
+
+Run from a clean worktree on Windows in a VS 2022 Developer PowerShell:
+
+```powershell
+$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
+$rev = git rev-parse HEAD
+if ($rev -ne 'a7847eae82aaac32175c9a7d199673d5c9323bcd') { throw "Unexpected source revision: $rev" }
+msbuild .\StereovisionHacks.sln /m /p:Configuration='Zip Release' /p:Platform=x64 /v:minimal
+$dll = (Resolve-Path '.\x64\Zip Release\d3d11.dll').Path
+Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $dll -Algorithm SHA256
+(Get-Item -LiteralPath $dll).VersionInfo | Format-List FileVersion,ProductVersion,OriginalFilename
+```
+
+The command is **documented but untested**. Keep the build log, tool versions (`msbuild -version`, `cl`), source revision, DLL SHA-256, and file version with the run record. Hash, not the often-generic PE version resource, is the definitive binary identity.
+
+After deploying the candidate through the normal isolated ZZMI test setup and starting the game, verify the DLL actually mapped into the game process. Replace the process name below with the observed game executable (elevate PowerShell if module enumeration is denied):
+
+```powershell
+$game = Get-Process -Name '<game-process-name>' | Select-Object -First 1
+$module = $game.Modules | Where-Object ModuleName -IEQ 'd3d11.dll'
+$module | Select-Object ModuleName,FileName
+Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $module.FileName -Algorithm SHA256
+(Get-Item -LiteralPath $module.FileName).VersionInfo | Format-List FileVersion,ProductVersion,OriginalFilename
+```
+
+Record the process ID and full mapped path. Compare the mapped-file SHA-256 with the just-built DLL hash; a matching filename alone proves nothing. Capture the active `d3dx.ini`, mod/profile identity, game build, importer/ZZMI version, and whether another wrapper or system `d3d11.dll` won module resolution.
+
+## Source audit and attribution boundary
+
+At the pinned tree, useful audit entrypoints are:
+
+- `DirectX11/CommandList.cpp`: `CustomResource::Substantiate()` (around 6407) is deferred until the actual device and binding flags are known; it enters resource-creation mode and dispatches file loading or resource creation. `LoadBufferFromFile()` (around 6471) calls `CreateFile`, `GetFileSize`, `malloc`, and `ReadFile` synchronously before buffer creation. Follow `LoadFromFile` and texture substantiation paths as well; this sample does not quantify call frequency or time.
+- `DirectX11/CommandList.cpp`: `CustomShader::substantiate()` (around 2613), called by `RunCustomShaderCommand::run()` (around 2819), creates shader and related state lazily. Audit this separately from shader replacement/deferred processing in `HackerContext.cpp` (`DeferredShaderReplacement*`).
+- `DirectX11/HookedDevice.cpp`: device hooks include SRV/UAV/RTV/DSV view creation. Trace `Create*View` callers and `CommandList.cpp` `CreateCompatibleView` (around 11902) / resource-view caches. Count resource and view creation independently.
+- `DirectX11/HackerDXGI.cpp`: `HackerSwapChain::Present` (around 192 onward) is the recurring frame boundary; it dispatches input, updates transitions, executes pending `ReloadConfig`, handles delayed initial reload, then draws overlay and calls the original Present. Instrument each stage and first correct rendered frame; do not label Present duration alone as asset load time.
+- `DirectX11/input.cpp`: `DispatchInputEvents`/key binding callbacks are the input path. Reload is deferred using `G->gReloadConfigPending`; trace request time, dispatch, reload start/end, and resulting visible frame.
+- `DirectX11/IniHandler.cpp`: `ParseResourceSections()` (around 2179) clears deferred bind flags, `customResourcePools`, and `customResources` before repopulating. `ReloadConfig()` (around 5159) holds `G->mCriticalSection` while clearing bindings/profiling state, saving persistent state, parsing INIs/includes, optimizing command lists, and deferring shader processing. It then resizes/binds INI resources and runs `InitIniParams()` via the context. Measure lock hold/wait and these subphases separately. Source confirms wide synchronization scope and destructive map rebuild; it does not establish that reload caused the observed activation delay.
+- Context/device lifecycle: `HackerContextFactory` and `HackerContext::~HackerContext()` in `HackerContext.cpp`, `HackerDevice::SetHackerContext()` in `HackerDevice.cpp`, device creation hooks in `HookedDevice.cpp`/`D3D11Wrapper.cpp`, and swapchain setup in `HookedDXGI.cpp`. Trace all device/context create, replacement, and teardown paths; associate all prepared device objects with that device lifetime. Confirm the actual ZZMI device and creation flags at runtime.
+
+Track separately: source/file reads and bytes, decode, shader compile/replacement, resource creation, view creation, command-list/config work, lock wait/hold, input dispatch, any readiness wait, activation request, and first correct complete frame. Include cache hit/miss, CPU/RAM/VRAM use, and frame-time p50/p95/p99/max. Images or captures must demonstrate correct output, since a fast but incomplete replacement is not a pass.
+
+## Repeatable paired ZZMI benchmark
+
+Use one fixed ZZMI/game build, profile, mod package, scene, character, outfit, camera, graphics settings, and storage setup. Record importer/game/DLL hashes, GPU/driver, CPU, RAM, VRAM budget, storage, and active config. Choose and document a known-correct mod with a repeatable visible replacement and a fixed save/route that reproduces the same scene. Use frame capture/video or an agreed frame marker to timestamp request and first complete correct appearance. Keep instrumentation identical between baseline and candidate and export traces off the render path.
+
+For each scenario, run matched baseline/candidate pairs from the same initial state, alternating order (AB/BA) to reduce order and thermal bias; restart the game between cold-start trials and do not silently clear or reuse caches. Use at least five pairs for initial cold-start investigation and at least 100 warm activations per qualified scenario for the plan’s initial p99 estimate; state uncertainty and sample size, record every trial, and report distributions rather than only the best run. Keep cold first use distinct from warm repeats. Run: (1) first appearance after load, (2) character revisit, (3) every available outfit toggle, (4) observed LOD transitions, (5) F10/config refresh with no file change, and (6) newly added mod. During preparation/gameplay capture frame-time traces and long stalls. Verify the final image/state for each pair. A setup-specific count of pairs is a measurement protocol, not a claim of statistical sufficiency for release.
+
+Separate preparation latency from request-to-visible activation latency. Preserve raw traces, run notes, and screenshots/video. Attribute a delay only when stage instrumentation explains it; otherwise label it unresolved. Report cache state, cold misses, coverage, reads/bytes, decode/compile/create counts, waits, and any delayed appearance without a visible freeze.
+
+## Evidence checklist and Phase 0 exit
+
+- [ ] Pinned source revision and clean worktree recorded; VS/MSVC/SDK versions and successful x64 `Zip Release` build log attached.
+- [ ] Built DLL hash/version and process-mapped DLL path/hash agree; active ZZMI config/profile/game/importer and device identity recorded.
+- [ ] Hardware, driver, RAM/VRAM budgets, and storage characteristics recorded.
+- [ ] Baseline paired trials and raw traces cover all six scenarios; known-correct rendering captures accompany each.
+- [ ] Per-stage timing and resource/view/shader counts support attribution, or each remaining source of delay is explicitly unresolved.
+
+**Exit gate remains blocked** until the Windows build and real game data above exist. This report provides audit leads and a repeatable collection procedure only; it contains no measured baseline, no verified artifact, and no gate verdict.
