+++ b/tests/d3d11_fixture/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# Standalone D3D11 baseline fixture (IM-02 / Phase 0)
+
+This Windows SDK C++17 console host runs real offscreen D3D11 draws without injection, a game, dependencies downloaded at build time, or changes to the main solution. It is a synthetic rendering corpus, **not ZZMI compatibility or in-game acceptance evidence**. The source baseline is `a7847eae82aaac32175c9a7d199673d5c9323bcd`; record the fixture diff/revision with every measurement.
+
+## Build and run on Windows
+
+Use an x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for Visual Studio 2022 with the C++ tools and Windows SDK installed. From the repository root:
+
+```bat
+cmake -S tests/d3d11_fixture -B work/d3d11_fixture-build -A x64
+cmake --build work/d3d11_fixture-build --config Release
+ctest --test-dir work/d3d11_fixture-build -C Release --output-on-failure
+work\d3d11_fixture-build\Release\d3d11_fixture.exe --output work\fixture-hardware-001 --repeat 30
+work\d3d11_fixture-build\Release\d3d11_fixture.exe --output work\fixture-warp-001 --repeat 30 --warp
+```
+
+Without CMake, from that same developer prompt:
+
+```bat
+mkdir work\d3d11_fixture-build
+cl /nologo /std:c++17 /EHsc /O2 /W4 /DUNICODE /D_UNICODE /DNOMINMAX /DWIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN tests\d3d11_fixture\main.cpp /Fowork\d3d11_fixture-build\main.obj /Fework\d3d11_fixture-build\d3d11_fixture.exe /link d3d11.lib dxgi.lib d3dcompiler.lib
+cl /nologo /std:c++17 /EHsc /W4 tests\d3d11_fixture\oracle_test.cpp /Fowork\d3d11_fixture-build\oracle_test.obj /Fework\d3d11_fixture-build\fixture_oracle_test.exe
+work\d3d11_fixture-build\fixture_oracle_test.exe
+work\d3d11_fixture-build\d3d11_fixture.exe --output work\fixture-hardware-001 --repeat 30
+```
+
+The output parent must exist; the output directory itself must be new. Exit 0 plus `PASS.txt` indicates a completed run; exit 1 or a missing marker means failure/incomplete evidence. Failed runs retain partial diagnostics. Hardware creation failure does not silently fall back to WARP. Do not compare WARP timing with hardware timing.
+
+## Corpus and actual checks
+
+Each of two device lifetimes creates VS/PS bytecode through `D3DCompile`, an input layout, immutable vertex/index buffers, a mutable tint constant buffer, immutable 2x2 RGBA textures, SRVs, a 64x64 RGBA render target/RTV, rasterizer state, and a staging readback texture. Two indexed triangles and one oversized triangle provide high/low LOD-like geometry covering the same area. Three distinct four-quadrant texture palettes and a dim toggle distinguish orientation, coverage, texture selection, and constant-buffer binding mistakes. Both shader compilation and resource/view creation are measured separately.
+
+The host draws a first use, then repeats revisit, outfit 1, dim toggle, low LOD, high LOD, and unchanged-state rebind scenarios. It prepares a third texture/view separately and draws a new generation plus its revisit. All device objects are then released and the entire sequence runs with a fresh device epoch. Driver/process caches may survive that recreation; run a separate process when collecting a process-cold sample. Rebind uses `ClearState` and normal rebinding; it does not execute importer F10. Generation selection changes a complete synthetic texture/view pair; it does not implement a cache or generation manager.
+
+Every draw is copied to staging, mapped, and checked against an independent CPU pixel oracle across all 4096 pixels and four channels. A channel error greater than 1 fails the run. The clear color has zero alpha so missing coverage fails. PPM files contain **actual mapped GPU RGB bytes**, exported only after successful comparison; alpha is checked but PPM cannot store it. No reference screenshots or runtime reports are checked in. Source data is generated in memory; no file-loading or image-decoding path is exercised.
+
+`frames.csv` records one row per draw with device epoch, scenario, iteration, outfit/toggle/LOD, submit CPU wall time, synchronous readback wait wall time, verification/copy CPU wall time, incorrect pixel count, maximum channel error, and host resource-creation count during submission. `stages.csv` records compile/device/resource/view preparation wall times and API object counts. Every successful warm draw has zero counted host creation calls. These counts describe only this executable's explicit API calls, not hidden driver work or the production loader.
+
+`metadata.txt` identifies the adapter, feature level, driver type, device creation flags, DXGI driver-version query result/raw value, dedicated video-memory size, UTC start, process ID, repetition count, source baseline and timing semantics. Preserve the executable hash (PowerShell `Get-FileHash`), source diff/revision, build/compiler/SDK version, OS build, CPU/RAM, driver version from `dxdiag`, power policy, and other GPU workload alongside the reports. DXGI may not provide a meaningful raw version on every driver; retain its query HRESULT. RAM/VRAM budget pressure and residency are not measured.
+
+Default `--repeat 30` produces 366 checked draws over two device epochs. Repetitions are limited to 1..1000. The workload and allocation sizes are fixed and small. Readback is intentionally synchronous to prove rendering completion; a lost/hung device is subject to the Windows driver's recovery behavior, with HRESULT failures reported. File export occurs outside the recorded frame intervals. There is no swapchain, Present, GPU timestamp query, game frame timer, first-eligible-frame cutoff, worker preparation, production trace integration, disk cache, or asynchronous activation. **Host QPC wall times, including readback synchronization, are neither GPU duration nor in-game frame time.** First use does not prove physical residency.
+
+For timing summaries, retain each raw row and compute p50/p95/p99/max per scenario **and per epoch/device type**, naming the percentile convention. Thirty repeats provide a smoke baseline, not a defensible p99 performance qualification. Use paired controls and production in-game capture before applying the implementation plan's proposed performance targets.
+
+## Portable verification and current evidence boundary
+
+The oracle is Windows-independent. A Linux C++17 compiler can check the pixel expectation, padded-row handling, corruption rejection, and input bounds:
+
+```sh
+c++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Werror tests/d3d11_fixture/oracle_test.cpp -o work/fixture_oracle_test
+work/fixture_oracle_test
+```
+
+This test does not compile or execute the D3D11 host. The implementation environment has no Windows SDK, Windows runtime, GPU, CMake, or cross compiler; no Windows build, GPU rendering, driver behavior, PPM baseline, or game performance was validated here. A Windows build/run is still required. The fixture cannot close the Phase 0 gate by itself.
+
+## Companion in-game capture protocol (manual; not executed here)
+
+1. Record the actual loaded DLL hash/path, importer version/configuration, game version, GPU/driver, device flags, budgets and storage. Build the pinned original source first and preserve its logs. Keep original mod content and quality unchanged.
+2. Capture a paired unmodified control and instrumented run with the same route/camera, mod set and settings. Start a fresh process for cold runs. Record request/input time, preparation completion, first eligible frame, first correct complete frame, long freezes and delayed appearance without a freeze.
+3. Visit a character for the first time, leave and revisit, cycle **every** outfit and toggle, cross each LOD boundary in both directions, invoke F10 unchanged, then add a new mod and repeat. Track real frame presentation separately from host CPU phase timings. Record omissions or unsupported behavior as unresolved; do not substitute the synthetic host for a game scenario.
+4. Preserve phase traces, capture provenance, frame-time rows, image comparisons against the known-correct game baseline, cache coverage/misses, bytes read, CPU/RAM/VRAM use and failures. Attribute each delay to a measured stage or label it unresolved. Do not infer successful first-use activation from resource-ready flags.
